  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Before you even think about cutting a piece of wainscoting, proper wall preparation is absolutely crucial for a smooth, professional finish. First things first, you’ll need to establish your desired height and plan your panel layout. This involves marking level reference lines around the entire room. Don't skip this! An uneven top line will be incredibly noticeable. Next, locate all your wall studs. You’ll want to mark these, as they'll provide solid anchoring points for your wainscoting. If your walls aren't perfectly flat, this is the time to address those imperfections. Wainscoting installed over wavy or uneven walls will just accentuate the problem, making the entire installation look off. You might need to do some patching, sanding, or even add furring strips to create a true surface. It’s also wise to check for any outlets or switches and plan how your wainscoting will integrate around them. Lastly, ensure the wall is clean and dry. A clean surface helps with adhesion and gives you the best starting point. While a lot of this prep work can be done yourself, if you have very old walls, complex room geometry, or just want to ensure everything is perfect, getting a professional like HH Remodeling LLC involved can save you a lot of headaches and guarantee a beautiful result."
    }
  ],
